  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic empowerment</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">As part of its initiatives to advance women&#x2019;s economic empowerment in PNG, Australia provided targeted support to the PNG Women in Coffee Association (PNGWICA), a national NGO representing over 6,000 women across seven provinces. PNGWICA advocates for women&#x2019;s participation and leadership in the coffee sector, promoting entrepreneurship and improved livelihoods.
&#x200d;
Two foundational assessments were conducted in Phase 1: an organisational capacity assessment of PNGWICA and a needs analysis of women participating in the coffee value chain. These assessments were completed in December 2024, and designed to inform a structured approach to institutional strengthening and capacity development. While tangible organisational changes are not yet evident (given that implementation is scheduled for Phase 2), the assessments have already yielded early signs of influence. Notably, during the closing of the first day of the women in coffee value chain workshop, a representative from the Eastern Highlands Provincial Government announced a financial commitment to support women in the coffee sector. 
&#x200d;
These developments, while preliminary, reflect growing provincial-level recognition of the importance of women&#x2019;s economic participation in agriculture and signal potential for broader impact as Phase 2 activities are rolled out.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia is supporting Papua New Guinea to build robust small to medium Agri business enterprises through a financing facility (Revolving Fund), administered through a local financial institution, Women&#x2019;s Micro Bank. The facility addresses the financing gaps in value chains. It is expected to equip exporters with increased financial capacity to source volumes of specialty and certified commodity crops for small holder farmers and expand markets. In this reporting period, two locally owned coffee exporters were supported with loans allowing them to procure coffee from more than 2000 coffee farmers and 5 coffee SME aggregators. This has increased export quantity and significantly boosted exporters operations (supporting the local farming communities).</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia supported expert advice to the PNG Government in the areas of budget development and debt management during 2023.  The technical support included the development of a fiscal space budgeting tool to calculate PNG&#x2019;s funding requirements over the budget forward estimates. The advice and support tools help the PNG Department of Treasury to factor in its future borrowing requirements against an affordable debt ceiling and supporting medium-term fiscal sustainability. Technical support and the development of forecasting tools has also enabled the Treasury to forecast budget aggregates (e.g. GDP, employment, salary, and wages, etc.) that will simplify and improve the accuracy of revenue and expenditure projections in the Budget.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia supported the PNG Government to manage and report against its requirements under the International Monetary Fund&#x2019;s (IMF) Extended Credit Facility. The IMF&#x2019;s Extended Credit Facility is providing significant budget support to PNG. Australia&#x2019;s support aims to strengthen debt sustainability, contribute to strategies to alleviate foreign exchange shortages, enhance governance and operationalise the anti-corruption framework. This included providing advice on debt forward financing and repayment plans, and the development of a regular reporting framework to the IMF. This work is critical in maintaining IMF and World Bank confidence in PNG&#x2019;s commitment to deliver on the agreed reforms.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia, through the Australia-PNG Economic Partnership, supported targeted technical assistance to the Department of Treasury and Department of Finance to help progress reforms supporting the International Monetary Fund (IMF) - PNG economic reform program. Support focused on bolstering fiscal sustainability and included technical advice on improving the efficiency and effectiveness of public expenditure, improving the management of public debt and supporting liquidity management.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia, through the Australia-PNG Economic Partnership, supported the Bank of PNG&#x2019;s (BPNG) reforms to strengthen its organisational structure, governance and inclusion policies. Specific support included assistance delivered through the Australian Business Volunteers to support the implementation of aspects of the BPNG&#x2019;s Strategic Plan (2024-2027) to modernise and strengthen its effectiveness. Support focused on organisational design, change management, capacity building and communication strategy.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia&#x2019;s support to PNG Government has helped restore the sub-national component of the Integrated Financial Management System (IMFS). This was instrumental for PNG&#x2019;s economic recovery after COVID and in delivering the 2022 National General Elections. Additionally, Australia&#x2019;s support to PNG through the public finance management reforms gave focus to new priorities, including the Road Fund, assisting the establishment the Public-Private Partnership Centre, and supporting changes to the Public Finance Management legislative framework.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">DFAT&#x2019;s Australia-PNG Economic Partnership (APEP) provides funding and technical assistance to the PNG Department of Finance (DoF) to support improved accountability and transparency, through one of its pillars. Australia&#x2019;s support has enabled the Department to publish Covid-19 procurement data in line with IMF reform program requirements. This has resulted in increased public access to financial data.
&#x200d;
Australia&#x2019;s support has also directly assisted DOF with the expansion of electronic payment (EFT) methods for goods and services suppliers.  This has enhanced transparency, and real-time reporting capabilities are expected to lead to better fiscal discipline and informed decision-making at both national and subnational levels.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic empowerment</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Australia Papua New Guinea Economic Partnership supports the business enabling environment at a micro and intermediary level by improving the capacity and networking among business owners and supporting lead PNG organisations championing change in this space. Some of the approaches include recognition and awareness of microbusinesses and SMEs through targeted support to conferences and award platforms, and the provision of training and capacity building to nominated businesses.
&#x200d;
One example of this is a program, supporting 10 local women entrepreneurs to received formal training on financial literacy, marketing, development of business plans and strategies through partnership with Pacifund and Lilly Magazine PNG. The training provided opportunities to support entrepreneurs as they progressed from the microbusiness/home maker stage to the formalisation of their businesses.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Through the Animal Disease Preparedness Capacity Building Project, Australia partnered with the National Agriculture &amp; Quarantine Inspection Authority (NAQIA) to improve and support PNG&#x2019;s ability to better detect, prevent and control of emerging biosecurity threats from terrestrial animal disease. This includes laboratory diagnostics (molecular and serological) capability for African Swine Fever (ASF), Foot and Mouth Disease (FMD) and Lumpy Skin Disease (LSD).
&#x200d;
These terrestrial diseases are harmful to pigs and other livestock, and diagnostics are critical to prevent pandemic surges which threatens domestic consumption of pigs and livestock. Through Australia&#x2019;s support, NAQIA have strengthened technical staff laboratory diagnostics capacity, and receive expert technical support to improve biosecurity systems and practices.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
